I've had a quick look at the code for router advertisments and immediatly found: `radv_configure` which has a guard clause calling `link_ipv6ll_enabled` (via `link_radv_enabled`).

This is some of the code for `link_ipv6ll_enabled`:
```c
bool link_ipv6ll_enabled(Link *link) {
        ...
        if (STRPTR_IN_SET(link->kind, "vrf", "wireguard", "ipip", "gre", "sit", "vti", "nlmon"))
                return false;
        ...
}
```

So systemd-networkd hardcodes that wireguard interfaces do not have an IPv6 link local address. Which is simply an incorrect assumption.

Edit: looking a bit further I've found a similar code path for the client side:

```
bool link_ipv6_accept_ra_enabled(Link *link) {
        ...
        if (!link_ipv6ll_enabled(link))
                return false;
        ...
}
```

@zx2c4 Can you give some explanation about **why** this needs to be reverted?

It seems like you are confirming that wireguard is strictly speaking not compliant with v6 of the internet protocol, but at the same time saying that this systemd feature should be reverted because it is strictly speaking not compliant with the wireguard protocol?

(I'm currently evaluating VPN options, and RAs happen to be one of my requirements. I'm open to suggestions that my network topology is wrong, and you seem to be of that opinion. For that reason, I'm interested in the why of it.)
